WEBVTT

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.987
How long would it take you to go all the way round the world?

00:00:11.251 --> 00:00:13.491
Today, about 44 hours.

00:00:14.508 --> 00:00:20.581
In November 2024, three men from Hong Kong went the whole way round on ordinary

00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:24.193
scheduled flights, on tickets anyone could buy.

00:00:27.663 --> 00:00:29.263
Higher up it is faster.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.526
The space station laps the planet every 90 minutes.

00:00:34.944 --> 00:00:38.315
The people on board watch 16 sunrises a day.

00:00:39.705 --> 00:00:41.497
Now take all of that away.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:43.583
No aircraft.

00:00:44.264 --> 00:00:45.395
No radio.

00:00:46.047 --> 00:00:50.036
In 1872 the honest answer was: nobody knew.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.593
So somebody turned it into a bet.

00:00:54.519 --> 00:00:58.445
80 days, half a fortune — and for the next 150 years,

00:00:58.445 --> 00:01:01.111
real people kept trying to beat him.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.731
Which is strange — because he never existed.

00:01:14.155 --> 00:01:18.187
The book came out, and people began treating a novel as a challenge.

00:01:20.157 --> 00:01:24.710
An American called George Francis Train insisted he had already done it,

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:29.010
two years before the book, and spent the rest of his life saying so.

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:39.265
Then in 1889 a New York reporter named Nellie Bly was given two days' notice,

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.194
one bag and a ticket, and sent east.

00:01:46.327 --> 00:01:50.124
On the way she stopped at Amiens and met Jules Verne himself.

00:01:51.995 --> 00:01:59.035
She came home in 72 days, 6 hours and 11 minutes — and beat a man who did not exist.

00:02:02.264 --> 00:02:07.392
What she did not know is that a rival magazine had put a second woman on a train the

00:02:07.392 --> 00:02:09.773
same day, going the opposite way round.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:16.058
Elizabeth Bisland lost by four days.

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:19.093
She also beat Fogg.

00:02:20.026 --> 00:02:21.221
It never stopped.

00:02:22.001 --> 00:02:27.882
In 1928 a Danish paper ran a competition in Verne's honour and sent the winner

00:02:27.882 --> 00:02:31.878
— a boy of 15 — round the world with aircraft banned.

00:02:36.297 --> 00:02:37.641
44 days.

00:02:38.484 --> 00:02:41.940
20,000 people met him at Copenhagen station.

00:02:43.668 --> 00:02:46.463
And there is a sailing trophy named after Verne.

00:02:47.914 --> 00:02:54.527
The first time anyone won it, the time was 79 days: under 80 by 17 hours.

00:02:57.578 --> 00:02:59.285
It now stands at 40.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.515
So who were they racing?

00:03:11.396 --> 00:03:12.356
A book.

00:03:13.017 --> 00:03:16.089
Around the World in Eighty Days, by Jules Verne.

00:03:17.588 --> 00:03:24.329
It ran as a serial in the Paris newspaper Le Temps, starting on 6 November 1872

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:29.023
— while, inside the story, Fogg was out there doing it.

00:03:33.503 --> 00:03:36.959
And Verne timed the ending to land on the real calendar.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.682
The instalments ran up to 20 December.

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:46.722
On the 21st — Fogg's deadline — there was nothing at all.

00:03:48.407 --> 00:03:51.607
The last two chapters appeared on the 22nd.

00:03:53.156 --> 00:03:55.823
Some readers thought it was actually happening.

00:03:57.161 --> 00:03:58.356
Bets were placed.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:02.694
Shipping companies wrote to Verne asking to be put in it.

00:04:04.396 --> 00:04:06.788
The book came out complete six weeks later,

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:09.068
and it has never been out of print since.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.480
Its hero spends the whole journey trying not to look at the world he is crossing.

00:04:25.987 --> 00:04:29.507
London, 2 October 1872.

00:04:31.538 --> 00:04:36.888
In a club on Pall Mall, Phileas Fogg bets five other members half of everything he

00:04:36.888 --> 00:04:41.586
owns that he can go round the Earth and be back in that room in 80 days.

00:04:46.066 --> 00:04:47.901
Here is the part that matters.

00:04:49.093 --> 00:04:50.949
He is not betting on himself.

00:04:52.152 --> 00:04:56.894
He is betting on the planet — on whether the railways and steamers and canals

00:04:56.894 --> 00:05:01.944
finished in the last three years have actually joined up into one working machine.

00:05:06.424 --> 00:05:08.877
His friends think the world is still too big.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.125
He thinks it has just closed.

00:05:13.275 --> 00:05:17.478
And in 1872 that was a genuinely open question.

00:05:19.848 --> 00:05:22.344
Which is why it is a bet and not a boast.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.987
Three years earlier the bet would have been impossible.

00:05:48.239 --> 00:05:49.242
Not hard.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:51.001
Impossible.

00:05:51.655 --> 00:05:58.567
May 1869: two railways meet in Utah, and America has a line from coast to coast.

00:06:01.097 --> 00:06:06.046
November: the Suez Canal opens, and ships stop going round Africa.

00:06:07.937 --> 00:06:12.673
1870: India's railways join up across the subcontinent.

00:06:14.494 --> 00:06:18.889
September 1871: a tunnel opens under the Alps.

00:06:20.599 --> 00:06:26.072
And on 22 August 1872 — six weeks before Fogg walks out of his front door

00:06:26.072 --> 00:06:30.869
— the last stretch of telegraph wire is strung across Australia,

00:06:30.869 --> 00:06:36.791
and for the first time a message can go the whole way round the world and back.

00:06:41.271 --> 00:06:44.556
The last inhabited continent joins the network.

00:06:45.906 --> 00:06:47.314
The circuit closes.

00:06:48.052 --> 00:06:49.481
And then he sets off.

00:06:50.226 --> 00:06:52.594
That circuit is the route of this series.

00:06:53.645 --> 00:06:58.262
Eight episodes, one lap: the Channel and the Alps, the canal at Suez,

00:06:58.262 --> 00:07:03.750
the railway across India with a piece missing, the ports of the East, the Pacific,

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.357
the line across America.

00:07:09.448 --> 00:07:13.501
And all the way round, the question Fogg is actually betting on.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.828
Had the world really closed?

00:07:17.671 --> 00:07:21.639
We are going to find out where it held — and where it very nearly didn't.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.117
It starts in London — so first, the London a visitor comes for.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:43.040
The clock tower at Westminster, and the bell inside it that everyone calls Big Ben.

00:07:45.213 --> 00:07:47.517
Strictly, Big Ben is only the bell.

00:07:48.649 --> 00:07:51.614
When Fogg left, the tower was 13 years old.

00:07:52.991 --> 00:07:58.730
The Houses of Parliament around it had been rebuilt after a fire in 1834.

00:08:01.133 --> 00:08:07.000
London Bridge — Fogg's London Bridge — was a stone bridge opened in 1831.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:15.118
In 1968 it was sold to an American businessman for about a million pounds,

00:08:15.118 --> 00:08:20.863
taken apart, shipped across the Atlantic and rebuilt in the Arizona desert.

00:08:25.343 --> 00:08:28.095
It still stands there, in Lake Havasu City.

00:08:29.392 --> 00:08:34.149
And Tower Bridge — the one on every postcard — Fogg never saw at all.

00:08:36.189 --> 00:08:40.328
It opened in 1894, 22 years after he left.

00:08:42.139 --> 00:08:46.101
So the city he set out from was brand new in some places,

00:08:46.101 --> 00:08:48.048
and not yet built in others.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.925
Fogg is setting out from the biggest city that had ever existed.

00:09:04.549 --> 00:09:08.709
London, 1871: 3.9 million people.

00:09:10.414 --> 00:09:12.206
A quarter bigger than Beijing.

00:09:13.099 --> 00:09:14.891
Two-thirds bigger than Paris.

00:09:15.785 --> 00:09:17.342
Five times New York.

00:09:18.155 --> 00:09:19.819
The world's largest port.

00:09:20.669 --> 00:09:24.808
12 main railway termini — no other city had anything close.

00:09:26.505 --> 00:09:29.835
And an underground railway, the first anywhere,

00:09:29.835 --> 00:09:34.228
nine years old and already carrying 9.5 million people a year.

00:09:37.153 --> 00:09:39.990
Here is what people get wrong about Fogg's London.

00:09:41.241 --> 00:09:42.457
It was not old.

00:09:43.154 --> 00:09:45.053
The Embankment was two years old.

00:09:45.983 --> 00:09:47.562
His station was eight.

00:09:48.383 --> 00:09:52.883
He set out from a brand-new city to find out whether the rest of the world had

00:09:52.883 --> 00:09:53.460
caught up.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.032
Everything left in this episode happens inside half a mile.

00:10:08.227 --> 00:10:12.344
Verne gives Fogg three addresses, all in the same corner of London.

00:10:14.579 --> 00:10:16.328
A house on Savile Row.

00:10:17.439 --> 00:10:18.975
A club on Pall Mall.

00:10:19.984 --> 00:10:21.861
A station at Charing Cross.

00:10:23.032 --> 00:10:27.789
He walks the first leg himself, and because he is the man he is, he counts it.

00:10:30.329 --> 00:10:32.740
1,151 steps.

00:10:34.165 --> 00:10:36.000
About 900 metres.

00:10:37.151 --> 00:10:39.391
That is the whole of London he uses.

00:10:40.735 --> 00:10:45.578
A man about to go round the Earth spends his last day on it inside half a mile.

00:10:48.158 --> 00:10:50.467
So we are going to walk that half mile

00:10:50.467 --> 00:10:55.571
— because all three of those places turn out to be more interesting than the man who

00:10:55.571 --> 00:10:56.179
used them.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.792
Fogg makes the bet in a club.

00:11:10.917 --> 00:11:13.178
It is worth knowing what that word meant.

00:11:14.525 --> 00:11:15.570
Not a bar.

00:11:16.343 --> 00:11:21.818
A private house the size of a palace, where a few hundred men who agreed with each

00:11:21.818 --> 00:11:27.359
other could eat, read, write letters and in many of them sleep — all of it staffed,

00:11:27.359 --> 00:11:28.695
none of it for sale.

00:11:33.175 --> 00:11:34.946
You could not buy your way in.

00:11:36.061 --> 00:11:38.920
You were proposed, seconded, then voted on.

00:11:40.549 --> 00:11:45.648
Every member dropped a ball into a box: white to admit, black to reject.

00:11:48.333 --> 00:11:50.402
Enough black balls and you were out.

00:11:51.658 --> 00:11:53.621
That is where blackballed comes from.

00:11:54.827 --> 00:11:58.781
At its height London had more than 400 of these places,

00:11:58.781 --> 00:12:03.168
most inside a few hundred metres of St James's and Pall Mall.

00:12:07.382 --> 00:12:12.266
This is the second leg of Fogg's walk, and almost every building on it is still

00:12:12.266 --> 00:12:12.822
standing.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:30.693
The room where Fogg makes his bet is, in real life, a political weapon.

00:12:32.769 --> 00:12:38.458
After the Reform Act of 1832, the Conservatives founded the Carlton Club,

00:12:38.458 --> 00:12:40.641
a few doors along Pall Mall.

00:12:43.933 --> 00:12:49.084
The other side wanted something to counter the machinations of the Carlton

00:12:49.084 --> 00:12:51.869
— their words — and in 1836 they got it.

00:12:55.185 --> 00:13:00.197
So the room where a fictional Englishman bets half his fortune on the size of the

00:13:00.197 --> 00:13:03.228
world is the headquarters of a political faction,

00:13:03.228 --> 00:13:06.321
built to out-build the opposition down the street.

00:13:10.801 --> 00:13:12.806
It is still there, still a club.

00:13:13.854 --> 00:13:20.083
In 1981 it became one of the first of the old clubs to admit women on equal terms.

00:13:22.746 --> 00:13:26.774
Its library, given book by book by its own members,

00:13:26.774 --> 00:13:29.381
runs to more than 85,000 volumes.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:42.560
Verne gives Fogg a house here — No. 7.

00:13:43.915 --> 00:13:46.582
Here is what he does not tell you about the street.

00:13:47.981 --> 00:13:51.949
280 metres, one block behind Regent Street.

00:13:53.895 --> 00:13:55.666
You can walk it in three minutes.

00:13:56.690 --> 00:14:02.861
The tailors arrived around 1803, following the army officers who already lived here

00:14:02.861 --> 00:14:08.957
— which is why the Savile Row shape comes from military uniform, not from fashion.

00:14:13.437 --> 00:14:16.980
The street will tell you the word bespoke was born here:

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:18.941
cloth spoken for by a customer.

00:14:21.532 --> 00:14:22.577
It was not.

00:14:23.295 --> 00:14:28.586
The word is from 1755, 50 years before the first tailor arrived.

00:14:31.088 --> 00:14:34.267
And in 2008 they lost it for real.

00:14:35.881 --> 00:14:41.538
The advertising regulator ruled that a machine-sewn suit made in Germany could still

00:14:41.538 --> 00:14:42.750
be called bespoke.

00:14:45.914 --> 00:14:47.514
Nobody owns the term.

00:14:48.466 --> 00:14:53.287
So the Row stopped defending the dictionary and started defending geography.

00:14:55.591 --> 00:15:00.051
Its own standard now demands a paper pattern cut for one person,

00:15:00.051 --> 00:15:05.556
50 hours of hand work, three fittings — and a workshop within 100 yards of this

00:15:05.556 --> 00:15:06.044
street.

00:15:10.524 --> 00:15:12.828
One last thing about Fogg living here.

00:15:14.075 --> 00:15:16.864
He leaves for three months with a single bag:

00:15:16.864 --> 00:15:19.344
two shirts and three pairs of stockings.

00:15:21.836 --> 00:15:25.759
He lived on the street where one suit takes 50 hours by hand

00:15:25.759 --> 00:15:28.897
— and he packed as though clothes did not exist.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:46.581
At the top of Fogg's street is the room where Britain worked out which parts of the

00:15:46.581 --> 00:15:48.016
world were still unmapped.

00:15:49.634 --> 00:15:54.492
The Royal Geographical Society moved into No. 1 Savile Row in 1870,

00:15:54.492 --> 00:15:57.248
from Burlington House round the corner

00:15:57.248 --> 00:16:01.091
— and this street was built on the Burlington estate,

00:16:01.091 --> 00:16:03.991
so it was a move of a few hundred yards.

00:16:07.464 --> 00:16:10.511
That is the room where expeditions to Africa,

00:16:10.511 --> 00:16:14.099
Asia and the South Pole were planned and argued over.

00:16:15.855 --> 00:16:22.043
In April 1874, David Livingstone's body lay in that building before it went to

00:16:22.043 --> 00:16:23.471
Westminster Abbey.

00:16:25.445 --> 00:16:30.143
Six doors down the same street, Verne had just put a man who bet he could go round

00:16:30.143 --> 00:16:32.378
the world without looking at any of it.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:38.595
One end of a three-minute street: the people who wanted to see everything.

00:16:39.853 --> 00:16:44.056
The other end: a man who crosses the planet and notices nothing.

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:48.841
The Society sold the building to a tailor in 1912

00:16:48.841 --> 00:16:53.505
— partly because that firm had dressed so many of the explorers.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:06.992
He begins a journey round the world from the exact spot Britain measures every

00:17:06.992 --> 00:17:07.888
distance from.

00:17:10.663 --> 00:17:11.879
The third address.

00:17:12.674 --> 00:17:14.914
A bronze horseman at Charing Cross.

00:17:16.143 --> 00:17:21.982
Since the early 1800s it has been the point every road distance to London is counted

00:17:21.982 --> 00:17:22.330
from.

00:17:25.231 --> 00:17:29.711
When a sign in Scotland says London 400, it means this statue.

00:17:31.889 --> 00:17:32.998
Not the city.

00:17:33.748 --> 00:17:34.921
This statue.

00:17:35.698 --> 00:17:39.709
There is a stone cross in the station forecourt that looks medieval.

00:17:41.688 --> 00:17:44.632
It is Victorian, and it is in the wrong place.

00:17:46.159 --> 00:17:48.719
The real one stood where the horseman is now.

00:17:50.084 --> 00:17:51.769
It was the last of 12.

00:17:52.763 --> 00:17:59.209
When Eleanor of Castile died in 1290, her body was carried 180 miles to Westminster,

00:17:59.209 --> 00:18:04.582
and Edward the First built a cross at every place it rested overnight.

00:18:09.062 --> 00:18:13.862
Charing was the final night, and its cross cost seven times any of the others.

00:18:16.175 --> 00:18:23.619
It came down in 1647, after standing 353 years, and the parish sold the stone

00:18:23.619 --> 00:18:26.906
— 47 cartloads of it — for paving.

00:18:31.386 --> 00:18:34.543
Fogg's train leaves from a station built on that spot.

00:18:36.161 --> 00:18:41.390
And at a quarter to nine, a man walks onto a platform and starts measuring something

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:42.262
rather larger.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.816
He gave himself 10 minutes to pack for the planet.

00:18:59.103 --> 00:19:02.767
That same morning Fogg had hired a new manservant:

00:19:02.767 --> 00:19:06.871
a Frenchman called Jean Passepartout — a former acrobat,

00:19:06.871 --> 00:19:12.441
rope-dancer and Paris fireman who had taken a valet's job because he wanted,

00:19:12.441 --> 00:19:14.932
in his own words, a tranquil life.

00:19:19.412 --> 00:19:21.460
He had been in the house nine hours.

00:19:22.473 --> 00:19:28.404
Fogg leaves the club at 7:25, tells him they are going round the world and will

00:19:28.404 --> 00:19:34.710
start in 10 minutes, and packs one carpet-bag: two shirts, three pairs of stockings,

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:40.116
a mackintosh — a rubberised raincoat — a cloak, and a railway timetable.

00:19:44.596 --> 00:19:47.540
The train for Dover leaves at a quarter to nine.

00:19:48.872 --> 00:19:50.067
It is raining.

00:19:50.775 --> 00:19:54.026
His watch is four minutes slow and set to London,

00:19:54.026 --> 00:19:57.410
and he has no intention of changing it for anybody.

00:20:00.063 --> 00:20:04.230
And somewhere behind them, in an empty house on Savile Row,

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:07.761
a gas lamp is still burning that nobody turned off

00:20:07.761 --> 00:20:11.434
— which is going to be funny for about 79 more days.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:18.319
Ahead of him: a boat across the Channel.

00:20:19.507 --> 00:20:22.750
A hole under the Alps that opened 13 months ago.

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:25.961
A canal three years old.

00:20:26.875 --> 00:20:29.627
A railway across India with a piece missing.

00:20:30.891 --> 00:20:33.131
An ocean that takes 22 days.

00:20:34.212 --> 00:20:39.480
And somewhere out on the Pacific, something quietly happens to the calendar that

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.060
nobody on board notices.

00:20:43.789 --> 00:20:44.920
80 days.

00:20:45.605 --> 00:20:46.949
He has already gone.
